From: sullivan@blaze.cs.jhu.edu (Gregory Sullivan)
Date: Thu, 27 Oct 94 10:32:06 EDT
Subject: [#94-10-539] BABY: Reproduction 

Nancy Lebovitz wrote:
>...
>What gripes me about all this is that I'm not convinced that being
>non-short is ameliorative. I find it a lot easier to think that the
>people who believe that short people are ridiculous are crazy.
>
>Sometimes I think that I'm spending half my life just shovelling
>garbage out of my head--the genetic modification I'd really like
>is not being suseptible to the nonsense floating around.

I think you have a valid gripe.  Social devaluation and stigma have been
associated with wide variety of cosmetic physiological attributes:  height,
weight, breast size, nose structure, vocal pitch, hirsuteness and many
others.  Desirable values for these attributes can vary between cultures and
can vary within a culture over time or within a subgroup.  Sociobiological
rationales can be offered for some preferences but many seem anachronistic,
arbitrary or capricious.  Yet, even if these preferences are nonsensical
they are still powerfully felt by some.  One response to baseless criticism
is to internalize it by forming a poor self-image. An alternative response
is to devalue the devaluers.

In the future I think advances in science and genetics will increase the
malleability of these physiological attributes.  Ultimately, perhaps they
will be changed like fashion accessories and thus will lose their current
psychological gravitas.      Gregory Sullivan


