Well, on the pragmatic level, it's a little inconveniant for me to
talk with people who are 6'+ if we're all standing. (I'm 4'11".)
If too high a proportion of the next generation is selected for
tallness, it could be a social problem for me--and it would only
get worse, since the point isn't attaining some absolute height,
it's gaining the advantage of being taller than the people around
you. If furniture, steps, shelves, etc. are set up for people
who are that much taller than I am, it's a problem. 

On the emotional level, I think I'm reacting to a message which
says "you shouldn't have existed--it's obvious that anyone would
want to edit your trait out of the species". It isn't just some
parents choosing for their children to be tall, but some writers
assuming that tallness is as desirable as intelligence or good
health.

 Part of this may be
an assumption that my genetic traits will always be bundled
together, so that I will never have an option about my height.
Damned if I know how I'd feel about it if it were some trait
like diabetes that's clearly a disease.

The future I'd like to see is one where people are choosing a
wild variety of physical types for all sorts of reasons.....
and where furniture and the like is adaptable to different
sized people. 

Nancy Lebovitz 

